    "id": "tpl-rss-6adf566b9dbc2b002c910e49",
    "title": "The Gravity Grand Prix",
    "organization": "Toronto Public Library",
    "provider": "Toronto Public Library",
    "description": "Get ready to design, build, and race! This high-speed engineering challenge invites young adults (competing in teams of 2 ages 18-24) to design the ultimate gravity-powered race car. Using professional 3D modeling software and cutting-edge 3D printing technology, your team will compete to see who can conquer the downhill track the fastest. The core of this challenge is physics: maximizing kinetic energy while minimizing friction. How will you transform potential energy into pure speed?🛠️ The Challenge Rules The Software: All cars must be fully designed from scratch using OnShape. A brief Introduction to the software will be provided. The Material Limit: To keep the playing field level, your final race car is strictly limited to a maximum of 30 grams of 3D printed filament and 2 tooth picks. Free Prototyping: Failure is part of engineering! Teams are encouraged to print, test, and redesign. Prototype prints are completely free throughout the design phase. The Track: A straight, downhill gravity track built for maximum acceleration. 📅 Timeline & Important Dates Design & Prototyping Week: Nov 4 to Nov 11— Access the DIH, collaborate with your partner, and test your prototypes. Race Day: Nov 11 (Exactly one week after design week) — Family and friends are welcome to watch the final showdown! 💡 Key Engineering Focus Areas to take home the trophy, your team will need to think critically about: Aerodynamics & Friction: How will you reduce air resistance and wheel friction to keep your car gliding? Weight Distribution: How can you strategically place your 30-gram limit to maximize downward momentum?",
