    "title": "Culture and Technology Book Club : Killed by a Traffic Engineer",
    "organization": "Toronto Public Library",
    "provider": "Toronto Public Library",
    "description": "The Culture and Technology Book club brings readers together every two months to discuss new and classic texts that raise important questions about the way technology transforms our cultural and political lives. This meeting we'll discuss: Killed by A Traffic Engineer (also available as an audiobook) Civil Engineering professor Wes Marshal takes a hard look at how traffic engineering standards in North America have shaped our streets for the worse. He examines some of the dubious and unscientific assumptions central to traffic engineering such as their fixation on speed. Marshal asks what it might look like to build transportation infrastructure with a more holistic understanding of people's needs. Register for this program to receive the discussion questions a week in advance of the meeting. Information Collection Notice: The personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act, sections 20(a), 20(c), and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registration, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca , 416-397-5981, 789 Yonge Street, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.",
