    "id": "tpl-rss-6ad78fc3e06996003a84bc1e",
    "title": "Doubles: A Panel Talk About the Film, Diversity & Indo-Caribbean Voices",
    "organization": "Toronto Public Library",
    "provider": "Toronto Public Library",
    "description": "Join us for an extraordinary evening with the actors and production team behind the six-time, award-nominated film Doubles , where diversity meets imagination, identity meets history and rarely heard Indo-Caribbean voices come together to celebrate the power of storytelling. The Canadian drama film, Doubles, set in both Trinidad and Toronto, explores the relationship between a son and his estranged father. When tragedy strikes, Dhani, a Doubles stand operator dreaming of owning his own restaurant, must leave Trinidad to find his father, whom he hasn't seen in years. Their reunion brings deeply held feelings to the surface as unexpected revelations change their lives forever. Presented by the Canadian Caribbean Indian Cultural Collective (CCICC) , this panel will feature: Mark Sirju , Producer Errol Sitahal , Actor portraying Ragbir Premika Leo , Actor portraying Skye Kenneth Mohabir , comedian, actor, and producer, will moderate the discussion. The film is approximately 1 hour 29 minutes long, followed by a discussion with the panelists about the importance of Indo-Caribbean representation, behind-the-scenes stories about the film, and an audience Q&A. Free program. Please register. Doors open at 1:45 pm. At 2:00 pm, seating becomes first-come, first-served until capacity is reached, with light refreshments (while supplies last). Organization Info: The Canadian Caribbean Indian Cultural Collective (CCICC) is a community organization representing Indians (currently referred to as South Asians) of various ethnicities, regions, and faiths, who are the descendants of the indentured labourers taken by the British to their colonies in the Caribbean and around the world. Their mission is to share their stories of struggle, resilience and contributions across the Caribbean and here in Canada. Information Collection Notice: The personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act, sections 20(a), 20(c), and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registration, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca , 416-397-5981, 789 Yonge Street, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.",
