    "organization": "Toronto Public Library",
    "provider": "Toronto Public Library",
    "description": "This program is part of the Toronto Reference Library's Entrepreneur in Residence (EIR) service. At some point the business has to become real on paper. This session will address the decisions that arise when choosing to operate as a sole proprietorship, a partnership or a corporation, as well as registering your business name in Ontario, understanding your obligations around HST, record-keeping and taxes. We'll also cover the protections new owners most often skip such as basic contracts, insurance, and how to protect your business name and brand. You'll leave knowing exactly which steps apply to you and roughly what each one costs. This session is general information, not legal or tax advice. The program includes a short networking component, providing participants with an opportunity to connect with other entrepreneurs. Registration recommended. Drop-ins welcome until room capacity is reached. To apply for one-on-one consultations with our resident entrepreneur, Ryan, please fill out the in-take form available at tpl.ca/eir . We begin accepting in-take forms on September 28, 2026 until available slots are filled. Information Collection Notice: The personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act, sections 20(a), 20(c), and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registration, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca , 416-397-5981, 789 Yonge Street, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.",
