    "title": "Bee-ing a Pollinator Protector: Plan Now for a Buzzing Spring Garden",
    "organization": "Toronto Public Library",
    "provider": "Toronto Public Library",
    "description": "Saving the bees is an environmental cause that resonates deeply with Canadians. While most of us might not be thinking about bees this time of year, our actions during the colder months can still support these pollinators, particularly native bees, which are crucial and threatened by climate change, habitat loss, and other pressures. In honour of the late bee scientist Sheila Colla, this presentation will offer practical tips on planting and maintaining gardens that provide beautifully blooming habitat for pollinators, with a particular focus on how native plants support wild bees. Presented by Lorraine Johnson , who co-authored A Garden for the Rusty-Patched Bumblebee with Sheila Colla, as part of TPL's environmental program series, Our Fragile Planet , supported by Friends of Toronto Public Library, South Chapter.​ Free. Register for a reminder. Doors open at 6:30 pm for attendees to mingle, chat with Lorraine, and explore related resources. Presentation begins at 7:00 pm. Place a hold or borrow from Toronto Public Library: A Garden for the Rusty-patched Bumblebee (book) A Garden for the Rusty-Patched Bumblebee (ebook) This program will also be offered virtually on Tuesday, November 3, 2026 , from 7:00 pm to 8:00 pm, as part of Climate Action Week. To register for the online program and for more info, see the listing . Information Collection Notice: The personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act, sections 20(a), 20(c), and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registration, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca , 416-397-5981, 789 Yonge Street, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.",
