    "id": "tpl-rss-6a8705bbdb86e4004d867404",
    "title": "The Delicious and Surprising History of The Dining Room: Author Talk with John Ota",
    "organization": "Toronto Public Library",
    "provider": "Toronto Public Library",
    "description": "John Ota presents​ and discusses his book The Dining Room . Explore the stories behind twelve iconic dining rooms in homes around the world—from a Japanese samurai house to Claude Monet's brilliant yellow dining room to Frida Kahlo's Casa Azul to Jackie Kennedy’s White House—and travel through time in pursuit of the ultimate design.​ The Dining Room is an enlightening and joyous celebration of history, culture and the art of dining. Presenter John Ota's charming observations of the surprising impact of dining rooms on our lives and the cherished moments we share within them will delight armchair travelers, history fanatics and interior design enthusiasts alike. John Ota has been involved with architecture and design since 1978, with degrees from Columbia University, the University of Toronto and the University of British Columbia. His bestselling book The Kitchen was a 2021 Taste Canada Awards finalist and a 2021 Paris Gourmand Cookbook finalist in the book design category. After working in architecture in Toronto, New York, and Vancouver, and as lead on multiple Ontario Ministry of Culture projects, John now loves to travel the world visiting historic houses, cooking regional food, and meeting people of all backgrounds.",
    "summary": "John Ota presents​ and discusses his book The Dining Room . Explore the stories behind twelve iconic dining rooms in homes around the world—from a Japanese samurai house to Claude Monet's brilliant yellow dining room to Frida Kahlo's Casa Azul to Jackie Kenned",
